 Please see our [Development guide](docs/development.md) for how to get Fizzy set up for local development.
 
 
+## Deploy to a single server
+
+Point a public hostname at your Linux Docker server. Allow inbound HTTP and HTTPS
+(ports 80 and 443) for Kamal Proxy to obtain a certificate and serve Fizzy. From
+an OSS checkout with the bundle installed and Docker with Buildx running locally:
+
+1. In [`config/deploy.yml`](config/deploy.yml), replace `fizzy.example.com` with
+   your hostname (no `https://`). Kamal uses it for SSH, HTTPS, and links in mail.
+   Make sure you can SSH to it as root, or set `ssh.user` to a Docker-enabled
+   user. Change `builder.arch` to `arm64` if deploying to an ARM server.
+2. Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` in the same file.
+   Use your provider's STARTTLS submission endpoint and a sender it accepts;
+   Fizzy sends sign-in codes by email.
+3. Copy `.kamal/secrets.example` to `.kamal/secrets`. Fill in your SMTP credentials
+   (leave both blank for an unauthenticated relay) and set `SECRET_KEY_BASE` to
+   the output of `bin/rails secret`. Keep this file private and reuse the same
+   key across deploys. For browser push, also add `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and
+   `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` to `env.secret` and the secrets file; see the
+   [key generation instructions](docs/development.md#web-push-notifications).
+4. Commit your non-secret configuration, then run `bin/kamal setup`. Visit
+   `https://your-hostname` and sign in. For later releases, run `bin/kamal deploy`.
+
+Kamal uses a local image registry, so no registry account is needed. The app
+container runs both Puma and the Solid Queue worker. Its named Docker volume,
+`fizzy_storage`, holds the SQLite databases and uploaded files in `/rails/storage`.
+Deploys reuse that volume. Back it up separately: it won't survive loss of the server.
+
+
